# yakogl/zephyr/Kconfig
#
# Kconfig for the YakoGL Zephyr module: the portable OpenGL ES core, one GPU
# backend (PowerVR BXS-4-64 or the i.MX8MP's Vivante GC7000) and the Zephyr
# OSAL.

config YAKOGL
	bool "YakoGL (OpenGL ES 2.0 on PowerVR BXS-4-64)"
	help
	  Build and link YakoGL: a source-compatible OpenGL ES 2.0 implementation
	  for the PowerVR BXS-4-64, using the Zephyr OSAL backend which wraps the
	  silicon-proven PowerVR bring-up driver (GPU memory + MMU, firmware boot,
	  KCCB submit/doorbell, completion wait, cache maintenance).

	  Requires the PowerVR bring-up driver (src/pvr_*.c) to be present in the
	  same build so the OSAL's driver-glue symbols resolve.

	  The GL/EGL core is Rust, built by cargo when the module is configured
	  (see zephyr/CMakeLists.txt for the CMake variables: cargo command,
	  RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN, -Z build-std, target triple and CPU, and
	  YAKOGL_RUST_LINKAGE=external for applications that link the yakogl
	  crate into their own Rust static library).

	  FPU: the Rust code is built for aarch64-unknown-none, a hard-float
	  target that uses the FP/SIMD registers anywhere, so CONFIG_FPU=y is
	  required (the build stops without it), and CONFIG_FPU_SHARING=y is
	  needed as soon as more than one thread uses the FPU (any thread that
	  calls GL/EGL does).

	  C library: YakoGL calls malloc, free, abort, memcpy, memmove, memset,
	  memcmp, strlen and snprintf: any Zephyr libc with a heap, e.g.
	  CONFIG_COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC with a large enough
	  CONFIG_COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE (shader sources and staging
	  buffers are on this heap). Floating-point printf (e.g.
	  CONFIG_PICOLIBC_IO_FLOAT) is only needed for the backend's debug
	  attribute dump. A Rust panic (a bounds or overflow check) logs the
	  message and calls abort().

if YAKOGL

choice YAKOGL_GPU
	prompt "GPU backend"
	default YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	help
	  Which GPU YakoGL generates command streams for. One backend and one
	  offline shader table are compiled into a build.

config YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	bool "PowerVR BXS-4-64 (Rogue XE, AM62P)"
	help
	  The PowerVR backend (src/pvr). Needs the PowerVR bring-up driver
	  (src/pvr_*.c) in the same build for the OSAL's driver glue and the
	  command generators the backend calls.

config YAKOGL_GPU_VIVANTE
	bool "Vivante GC7000UL (i.MX8MP)"
	help
	  The Vivante backend (src/vivante): LOAD_STATE command streams for the
	  GC7000's front end, with no firmware. See src/vivante/VIVANTE_NOTES.md
	  for what is implemented and what the hardware still has to answer.

config YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	bool "Broadcom V3D 4.2 (Raspberry Pi 4)"
	depends on YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE
	help
	  The V3D backend (src/v3d): binning and render control lists built by
	  the job builder in driver/src/v3d, submitted by the OSAL in
	  src/osal/zephyr_v3d.c, which drives the V3D itself (power, MMU,
	  submit) through driver/src/v3d/v3d_hw.c. The in-process compiler is
	  this GPU's only shader back end: there is no offline table for it.
	  docs/online-shaderc/v3d-backend.md, "M6", says what is drawn and what
	  is refused by name.

endchoice

config YAKOGL_PVR_POOL_RECLAIM
	bool "PowerVR: reuse freed GPU memory as soon as no kick can read it"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	default y
	help
	  The Zephyr OSAL holds every freed block until the kick after the
	  last one submitted completes, even when nothing is being recorded:
	  memory an application deletes between two scenes cannot be reused
	  while it sets up the next one (glmark2's terrain,
	  driver/docs/pvr-render-pool.md, mechanism 2). With this, a free made
	  while no frame is open waits only for the last submitted kick
	  (pvr_backend_free_fence). An allocation the pool refuses first gets
	  back, without waiting, what completed kicks held, and is tried again.
	  If it is still refused, it waits for the submitted kicks (bounded by
	  the backend's own submit wait; the allocating GL call blocks for
	  it), gets back what they held, and is tried once more. On by
	  default: without it glmark2's shadow depth texture (1600x1200) was
	  refused beside 23932 KiB of held frees and its framebuffer read
	  GL_FRAMEBUFFER_INCOMPLETE_ATTACHMENT (AM62P, 2026-10-04; gl_test 178
	  heldfree reproduces it). n keeps the old hold, for measurement.

config YAKOGL_PVR_FRAME_SETS
	int "PowerVR: frame sets (kicks recorded ahead of the GPU)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	range 1 4
	default 4
	help
	  How many sets of per-kick resources the backend builds: each is a
	  GENERAL arena, a PDS arena, a 64 KiB VDM stream and small tables,
	  and the next kick is recorded into one while the GPU reads the
	  others. At the defaults, 4 x (1024 KiB GENERAL + 768 KiB PDS), this is
	  7488 KiB of the render pool: the arenas are 2.0 and 2.4 times
	  glmark2's largest frame on the AM62P, measured
	  (driver/docs/pvr-render-pool.md; 4 x (4096 + 1024) took 20800 KiB).
	  Another configuration is a measurement until the regression set has
	  run with it; judge it by "pool owner backend frame arenas high-water"
	  (YAKOGL_PVR_POOL_OWNERS). A frame that overflows an arena is split,
	  and a draw that does not fit an empty frame is refused, naming the
	  arena.

config YAKOGL_PVR_ARENA_GEN_KB
	int "PowerVR: each frame set's GENERAL arena, KiB"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	range 16 4096
	default 1024

config YAKOGL_PVR_ARENA_PDS_KB
	int "PowerVR: each frame set's PDS arena, KiB"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	range 8 1024
	default 768

config YAKOGL_PVR_POOL_OWNERS
	bool "PowerVR: print the render pool's live memory by owner at run end (a measurement)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_POWERVR
	default n
	help
	  At the end of a run (osal_zephyr_diag_print()), one line per owner
	  of live pool memory: "pool owner <name>: N blocks, K KiB (heaps)" for
	  the driver (render statics, freelists, render context, HWRT sets),
	  the backend (frame sets with their arenas' high-water marks, statics,
	  depth/stencil) and the GL core (buffers, textures, renderbuffers,
	  default surfaces, programs, draw staging). Then one balance line
	  against the pool's live bytes, with what no owner claims printed as
	  "unaccounted" (driver/docs/pvr-render-pool.md, the fixed ~36 MiB).
	  Off by default; it walks every table once, at the end.

config YAKOGL_GC_POOL_NO_REUSE
	bool "GC7000: never reuse freed GPU memory (a measurement; leaks)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_VIVANTE
	default n
	help
	  A freed block never goes back to the pool: every allocation comes
	  from the bump, at clean ascending addresses, as gcgpu.ko's
	  pool_no_reuse does on Linux. The pool's block reuse has an open
	  defect inside a single client's run (gc_render.c, above
	  gc_render_pool_reset). It leaks everything freed;
	  gc_osal_no_reuse_leaked_kib() says how much, so a run that ran the
	  carveout dry is recognisable.

config YAKOGL_GC_LOG_BIG_ALLOCS
	bool "GC7000: log the callers of the first 16 allocations of 1 MiB or more (a measurement)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_VIVANTE
	default n
	help
	  printk's the size and the two return addresses of each, so
	  addr2line on zephyr.elf names who allocates a whole-screen buffer.

config YAKOGL_GC_ALLOC_PAGE_ALIGN
	bool "GC7000: page-align GPU allocations of 4 KiB or more (a measurement)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_VIVANTE
	default n
	help
	  Every GC7000 allocation is otherwise 64-byte aligned (GC_OSAL_ALIGN),
	  render targets and pbuffers included, while every colour target
	  Mesa's etnaviv hands this GPU is a page-aligned GEM buffer, and the
	  core's PE cache lines are 256 bytes (CACHE128B256BPERLINE). This
	  aligns every allocation of 4 KiB or more to 4 KiB, which is what a
	  GEM buffer would be. Off until measured: dEQP color_clear on the
	  i.MX8MP fails 8 of 19 in the group image and not in a single-case
	  one, deterministically, which points at an address; two images from
	  one commit, this off and on, are the A/B.

config YAKOGL_GC_CARVEOUT_FILL
	bool "GC7000: fill the GPU pool with a fixed byte at boot (a measurement)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_VIVANTE
	default n
	help
	  kexec does not clear the carveout, so memory the GPU reads without
	  our having written it holds whatever the previous boot left there.
	  Every pool allocation is zeroed when it is handed out
	  (gc_render_alloc), so this only reaches reads OUTSIDE every live
	  allocation: past an allocation's end, or past the bump. With it on,
	  the whole pool (GC_OFF_POOL to the carveout's end; the MMU tables
	  below it are left alone) is set to YAKOGL_GC_CARVEOUT_FILL_BYTE
	  before the GPU starts, so such a read gives the same answer on every
	  boot. A result that flips between runs and stops flipping with this
	  on is an out-of-allocation read; one that still flips is not.

config YAKOGL_GC_CARVEOUT_FILL_BYTE
	hex "The byte YAKOGL_GC_CARVEOUT_FILL writes"
	depends on YAKOGL_GC_CARVEOUT_FILL
	range 0x0 0xff
	default 0xa5
	help
	  0xa5a5a5a5 is -2.87e-16 as a float and not a plausible colour, depth
	  or command word; 0x00 is the other arm (a read that passes on zeros).

config YAKOGL_V3D_DRAWS_PER_JOB
	int "V3D draws one job holds"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default 1024
	range 64 8192
	help
	  The draw budget the V3D backend sizes one job's lists for: the
	  binning list (4 KiB + 129 bytes a draw), the indirect buffer (8 KiB +
	  440 bytes a draw; both 15 % over Slint's measured 112 and 382) and
	  the binner's tile pool (Mesa's rule, capped at 512 KiB). A frame with
	  more draws is split into more jobs, which is correct but costs a load
	  and store of the whole target per extra job.

config YAKOGL_V3D_BIN_OVERFLOW_BLOCKS
	int "V3D binner overflow blocks (256 KiB each)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default 2
	range 0 64
	help
	  Memory the OSAL gives the binner when a job's tile pool runs out
	  (the OUTOMEM interrupt), one 256 KiB block per request, as the Linux
	  driver does. The blocks are taken from the carve-out once, at init,
	  and serve every job again. Slint's 493 draws used 282,624 bytes of
	  the 663,552-byte pool, so two blocks cover about 1,024 draws twice
	  over. When the blocks run dry, the job fails by name ("binner out of
	  memory") and is not retried.

config YAKOGL_V3D_SPLIT_ZS
	bool "V3D: depth and stencil kept apart by renderbuffer"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default y
	help
	  Keep an FBO's depth and stencil each under its own renderbuffer,
	  loaded and stored apart (the backend's v3d_split_zs), instead of one
	  buffer per pair of names. Two FBOs sharing a depth renderbuffer then
	  share its depth whatever their stencils (dEQP
	  fbo.render.shared_depthbuffer), and an FBO whose stencil is replaced
	  keeps its depth (recreate_stencilbuffer). On since the board
	  measured it: gl_test 123 zsapart passes both halves, and dEQP FB2
	  on 41719f83 went from 301 to 314 of 319 with it, 13 cases moved and
	  none regressed (2026-09-25). n keeps one buffer per pair of names.

config YAKOGL_V3D_S8_STENCIL
	bool "V3D: stencil halves in S8 buffers (1 byte a pixel)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D && YAKOGL_V3D_SPLIT_ZS
	default n
	help
	  Keep each stencil half (a stencil-only framebuffer's stencil, and
	  the stencil of a framebuffer whose depth and stencil renderbuffers
	  differ) in an S8 buffer of its own, loaded and stored as buffer
	  STENCIL with image format S8, as Mesa's v3d does for the separate
	  stencil of a Z32F_S8X24 zsbuf (v3dx_rcl.c:72-160), instead of a
	  D24S8 buffer: a quarter of the memory. Mesa never makes a
	  stencil-only S8 zsbuf, so this job shape is ours; opt-in until the
	  board has measured it (the cluster's femtovg layers, gl_test 123,
	  162, 167). Single-sampled only; needs YAKOGL_V3D_SPLIT_ZS.

config YAKOGL_V3D_PACK_SKIP
	bool "V3D: write a draw's state packets only when their inputs changed"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default y
	help
	  The job builder (driver/src/v3d/v3d_job.h "Packet skip") packs and
	  writes a group of state packets only when it was not written yet in
	  the job or its inputs differ from the ones it was last written with,
	  as Mesa's v3dX(emit_state) does with its dirty flags. POINT_SIZE and
	  the draw's own packets are written on every draw. Off: every state
	  packet on every draw, as before. On by default since the Pi 4
	  regression set ran with it (job_check part 15 pins both states;
	  GTPACK gl_test 18/18; PACKA/PACKB frame-300 emit 4.40 -> 3.07 us/draw
	  with 79.7% of packets skipped and equal crc; knob-on dEQP
	  fragment_ops.blend 1060/0, fragment_ops.depth_stencil 621/0,
	  rasterization 49/0 (matching the knob-off control)); the counts are
	  printed in the OSAL's close record and the cluster demo's periodic
	  lines. Turn it off (n) to compare against the old always-write path.

config YAKOGL_V3D_TFU
	bool "V3D: tile large textures with the texture formatting unit"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default n
	help
	  Tiled copies of at least YAKOGL_V3D_TFU_MIN_BYTES are made by the
	  V3D's texture formatting unit from the GL core's storage instead of
	  by the CPU: 2.2 ms against 145 ms for a 1920x720 RGBA8 image on the
	  Pi 4 (gl_test case 140, records/pi4/record_TFU140_gl_test_tfu140_9b55d8df.txt).
	  RGBA8, L8/A8 (R8) and LA8 (RG8), one level. A job the TFU does not
	  take, or that fails, is tiled on the CPU as before. Off by default
	  until a DEMO has measured it.

config YAKOGL_V3D_TFU_MIN_BYTES
	int "V3D: the smallest tiled image the TFU makes (bytes)"
	depends on YAKOGL_V3D_TFU
	default 65536
	range 4096 268435456
	help
	  Below this the CPU tiles, which costs no job. The default is a
	  placeholder until case 140's small-size timing measures the TFU's
	  fixed cost per job.

config YAKOGL_V3D_TILED_COPIES_BYTES
	int "V3D tiled texture copies kept across jobs (bytes)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default 8388608
	range 1048576 268435456
	help
	  The most the V3D backend keeps of textures tiled for its texture unit,
	  across jobs (the least recently sampled copy goes first). A texture
	  whose tiled image is larger than this, and larger than the per-job
	  texture arena (8 MiB), is refused by name: a 2048x2048 RGBA8 texture
	  tiles to about 16.8 MiB, so an image that samples one needs about
	  24 MiB here, and a carve-out that holds it.

config YAKOGL_V3D_CARVEOUT_BYTES
	int "V3D carve-out (bytes)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default 50331648
	help
	  The memory the V3D draws from: a static of the image, mapped Normal
	  non-cacheable, with the GPU's page table (512 KiB) at its front. It
	  holds every buffer, texture, shader, control list and the binner's
	  tile memory (about 1.4 MiB of the backend's own). The image's RAM
	  (the board's sram0 node) must hold it besides the kernel.

config YAKOGL_V3D_CARVEOUT_TOP_BELOW
	int "V3D: carve-out blocks below this size come from the top (bytes, 0 = off)"
	depends on YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	default 0
	range 0 268435456
	help
	  The carve-out's allocator (src/osal/v3d_granules.h) places a block
	  smaller than this (rounded down to whole 256-byte granules) by last
	  fit, the highest free block, and larger ones by first fit from the
	  bottom. Small short-lived blocks (glyph and upload tiled copies,
	  vertex buffers) then gather at the top and leave one long free run
	  in the middle for a large block. The host replay of STRESS1D
	  (tools/v3d/osal_granules_check.c) at 1048576: the longest free run
	  after the upload steps 6,189,056 bytes against 3,862,528 with first
	  fit, and fewer map reads a call. Alignment, capacity and the
	  refusal are unchanged. 0: first fit for every block, as before.
	  Opt-in until the regression set has run with it; the close record's
	  "carve-out find" line prints the setting, the free runs and the
	  longest.

choice YAKOGL_SHADERC
	prompt "shader back end"
	default YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE
	help
	  Where a shader's machine code comes from. The GL core asks the back
	  end named here; only one is in a build.

config YAKOGL_SHADERC_OFFLINE
	bool "offline table (Mesa reference; measurement images only)"
	help
	  MEASUREMENT ONLY. The Mesa reference table (tests/reference/blobs.rs
	  or vivante_blobs.rs: what Mesa's pco_shc / etna_shc made of the
	  source list), linked as the image's shader table: glShaderSource
	  text is matched by SHA-256 against it. It exists for A/B images
	  against the in-process compiler (gl_test 193..195's "table image");
	  it selects the
	  crate's `mesa-reference-table`, which no other build links. It will
	  be replaced by the table our own compiler precompiles at build time
	  (tools/precompile). An application whose shader is not in the table
	  cannot draw with it.

	  The table is the largest single thing the library can carry: 258,000
	  bytes of GC7000 instruction words, about 6.9 MB of PowerVR, before
	  the reflection rows.

config YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE
	bool "in-process compiler"
	help
	  The default. Compile the shader on the target, from the GLSL ES text
	  the application passes to glShaderSource, with no table and no host.

	  This links no table at all (no `mesa-reference-table`; the crate's
	  `no-shader-table` as well), which is the point: an image carrying a
	  compiler does not also need the bytes it was meant to replace.

	  The compiler is no_std and allocation-free; it works in the region
	  sized by YAKOGL_SHADERC_SCRATCH_BYTES below.

	  STACK: the compiler runs on the thread that calls glCompileShader or
	  glLinkProgram, and that thread needs 256 KiB of stack
	  (docs/online-shaderc/frontend.md, "Stack"). Measured on the host at
	  104 KiB for the worst compile, almost all of it the code generator;
	  a 64 KiB main-thread stack overflowed into the idle thread on the
	  AM62P. Size CONFIG_MAIN_STACK_SIZE, or the stack of whichever thread
	  compiles, accordingly.

config YAKOGL_SHADERC_PRECOMPILED_ONLY
	bool "precompiled shaders only (no compiler)"
	select YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_SHADERS
	help
	  The image carries the shaders our compiler made of the source list
	  at build time (YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_SHADERS, which this selects) and
	  no compiler: no front end, no scratch or keep region
	  (zephyr_shaderc_scratch.c is not linked), no compile stack. A
	  shader the table holds is looked up; anything else is refused by
	  name, -ENOENT, with the line in the info log (and on the console
	  once per cause):
	    precompiled shaders: <vertex|fragment> shader sha256=<hex> is not
	      in this image's precompiled shaders and the image has no shader
	      compiler; add it to <list> and rebuild
	    precompiled shaders: <name> has no variant for blend key <hex>
	    precompiled shaders: program <vs>/<fs> was not linked at build time
	  A program links when the table linked that pair (or when neither
	  stage declares a uniform and the fragment shader reads no varying,
	  so that no rule of the compiler's link could have said anything).
	  Not on the V3D (its GPU choice needs the compiler).

endchoice

config YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_SHADERS
	bool "precompiled shaders in front of the compiler"
	depends on (YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE || YAKOGL_SHADERC_PRECOMPILED_ONLY) && !YAKOGL_GPU_V3D
	help
	  With the in-process compiler (YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE): compile the source list (tools/shaders/precompiled.list, or
	  YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_LIST) with this tree's own compiler while the
	  image is built (yakogl-precompile, a host program cargo builds into
	  the build directory first), and link the table it writes in front of
	  the in-process compiler: a shader the application compiles is looked
	  up there, by the key the compiler's cache uses, before it is
	  compiled, and a hit runs no compile (shc_precompiled_hits() counts
	  them; shc_online_compiler_runs() does not move). A miss compiles as
	  without the table. The table's toolchain mark is logged at the first
	  look-up; a table for another GPU or ES level does not build. The
	  crate's `precompiled-shaders`. Adds the table's rows to the image
	  (the whole list on the PowerVR is about 3.8 MB of code; one app's
	  much less, YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_APPS). Without a compiler
	  (YAKOGL_SHADERC_PRECOMPILED_ONLY, which selects this) the table is
	  the whole back end.

config YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_APPS
	string "the list's apps to precompile (space-separated; empty: the whole list)"
	depends on YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_SHADERS
	default ""
	help
	  `app` names of the source list, e.g. "gl_test_es100" or "femtovg":
	  only those programs and their sources go into the table
	  (yakogl-precompile --app). Empty: every listed source and program.

config YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_LIST
	string "the source list (empty: tools/shaders/precompiled.list)"
	depends on YAKOGL_PRECOMPILED_SHADERS
	default ""
	help
	  A source list in tools/shaders/precompiled.list's syntax, its paths
	  relative to its own directory.

config YAKOGL_SHADERC_SCRATCH_BYTES
	int "compiler scratch region (bytes)"
	depends on YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE
	range 65536 268435456
	default 4194304
	help
	  Bytes the in-process compiler works in: tokens, the AST, the IR, the
	  register allocation. The region is CALLER-LENT -- this Kconfig sizes a
	  static in the OSAL and lends it to the compiler -- so a shader needing
	  more than it holds is REFUSED BY NAME, with the shader and both
	  numbers in the message. That is a diagnosable failure rather than an
	  image that does not boot, which is why the size is a policy here and
	  not a constant in the compiler.

	  The lower bound of the range mirrors MIN_SCRATCH_BYTES in
	  src/shaderc/online/store.rs, which is the authority: the compiler
	  refuses a smaller region outright, so this range only moves the
	  refusal from boot to configure time. A mismatch is not silent -- the
	  lend fails and the OSAL logs both numbers and stops the init.

	  4 MiB (since 2026-09-25; 2 MiB before) is the size proven on the
	  host: at 2 MiB dEQP-GLES2 light_amount.16omni's vertex shader (3 MiB)
	  and uniform_api.random.23's fragment shader (4 MiB) were refused on
	  the PowerVR online back end. It is not a bound derived from
	  any measurement, and no measurement can give one: the corpus is what
	  WE compile, not what an application will.

	  What is measured is how deep the corpus has gone. At the time of
	  writing (2026-09-22, commit d52dda5) tests/scratch_survey.rs reports a
	  peak of 504,400 bytes over the GC7000 corpus, set by
	  fill_image_gradient_conic.glyph.nodiscard.frag.

	  That number moves, in both directions, and quickly. Readings taken the
	  same day: 931,496, then 447,496 when the front end's lowering was
	  rewritten, then 515,472 when if-conversion let more sources reach the
	  allocator, then 504,400 when a larger MAX_VREGS let the deepest of
	  them compile instead of being refused. Treat any single figure as
	  "what the compiler did that hour", not as a requirement.

	  To re-measure:

	    cargo test --no-default-features \
	        --features host-test,gpu-vivante,shaderc-online \
	        --test scratch_survey -- --nocapture

	  Lower this knowingly on a constrained image: the failure it buys is
	  named and reported, but it is still a shader that will not compile.

config YAKOGL_SHADERC_KEEP_BYTES
	int "compiled shader region (bytes)"
	depends on YAKOGL_SHADERC_ONLINE
	range 65536 268435456
	default 1048576
	help
	  Bytes the in-process compiler keeps compiled shaders and programs
	  in: their code, uniform tables and sources, and the draw-time
	  variants, for as long as the application holds them. CALLER-LENT
	  like the scratch region above: this sizes a static in the OSAL. A
	  compile that does not fit is refused by name ("online shaderc: keep
	  full on this ... shader: N bytes free in its largest run, U of S
	  bytes in use; raise ..."), and every board run prints the region's
	  high-water ("shaderc: keep high-water ...").

	  It must hold every shader and program the application keeps alive
	  AT ONCE, not every one it ever compiled: glDeleteShader and
	  glDeleteProgram give the memory back. The largest measured is the
	  AGL cluster (Slint with femtovg) on the AM62P: 564,544 bytes at its
	  high-water, nothing refused. The default, 1 MiB, holds that with
	  484 KB to spare; raise it for an application that keeps more.

	  The lower bound mirrors MIN_KEEP_BYTES in store.rs, which is the
	  authority: a smaller region is refused at boot with both numbers.

config YAKOGL_REPORT_ES2
	bool "report OpenGL ES 2.0 rather than ES 3.0"
	default y
	help
	  glGetString(GL_VERSION) says "OpenGL ES 2.0" and
	  GL_SHADING_LANGUAGE_VERSION "OpenGL ES GLSL ES 1.00", the ES 3.0-only
	  queries are GL_INVALID_ENUM, and the ES 2.0 rules that ES 3.0 dropped
	  apply (a non-power-of-two texture has no mip chain). This is what a
	  conformance build needs.

	  On by default: YakoGL reports OpenGL ES 2.0 until the ES 3.0 work has
	  progressed far enough to make 3.0 the default. A build that needs an
	  ES 3.0 context (Qt Quick 3D's RHI, a dEQP-GLES3 image, gl_test's
	  "#version 300 es" cases, Slint/FemtoVG) says CONFIG_YAKOGL_REPORT_ES2=n.

config YAKOGL_TEST_KNOBS
	bool "the test knobs: C entry points a test suite sets (never in a product)"
	default n
	help
	  The crate's `test-knobs` feature: the switches and probes an
	  on-silicon suite sets to run an experiment's arms -- today the
	  GC7000's gc_debug_set_msaa and its scratch probes,
	  gc_debug_set_flip_experiment and gc_debug_set_narrow_la. Without it
	  none of them is in the library and the backend behaves as each
	  knob's default. The gl_test, gc_gl_test and deqp samples select it;
	  a product image leaves it off (tools/check.sh, "no test knob in the
	  artefact"). C that calls a knob tests CONFIG_YAKOGL_TEST_KNOBS.

module = YAKOGL
module-str = YakoGL
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # YAKOGL
